WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.132
ERIC ANDERSON: So
glad to have you here.

00:00:06.132 --> 00:00:09.140
As you know, we announced
Data Prep this morning,

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.380
and so I had to obscurely
title this talk.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.310
I mean maybe it is
a fitting title,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.289
but it couldn't be as
descriptive as I wanted.

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:18.080
So maybe you are the
few that actually knew

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.520
what you were coming to today.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.770
Maybe you read, you
understood the code.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.620
If so, congratulations.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:25.380
Yes.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.180
So I am I'm Eric Anderson,
I'm the product manager

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.970
for Cloud Data Prep.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.940
So we announced it this morning
for the first time publicly,

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.875
which I'm super excited about.

00:00:36.875 --> 00:00:38.750
I can finally tell my
wife what I do at work.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.370
No, it's not that bad.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.290
And she is here tonight, so
you can give her a hard time.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.380
So Cloud Data Prep, as you
know or as you may have heard,

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.220
is intelligent, visual, and
server-less data preparation

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:57.090
interface that runs on
Google Cloud's data services.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.150
A quick overview on what
we're going to cover today,

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.470
and maybe to recap
the announcement.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.920
It is available today
in private beta,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.170
which means to select users.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.960
And then we plan in the coming
weeks for this to be available,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.966
I'm sorry weeks or months--

00:01:11.966 --> 00:01:13.590
I don't want to
promise anything or get

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.250
you set to expectations--
is to be available publicly,

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.630
in a public beta.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:23.000
We're going to start with the
need for data preparation.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.840
Then go into the two benefit
pillars for data prep.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.590
So first, the visual and
interactive interface that

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.650
we've described previously and
you saw today, this morning,

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.720
as well as the
server-less aspects--

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.830
how these are executed without
any configuration on your part

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.140
at basically any scale.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.390
And then we'll wrap up
with a couple examples,

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:44.920
and spend most of
our time, which

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.050
is where I think you'd like
it, in a demonstration.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.459
So the need for
data preparation.

00:01:51.459 --> 00:01:53.500
And I'm sorry to pull out
the same numbers you've

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.290
seen before, but it's
part of the routine.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.390
You have to do this.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:04.180
As you may know, or may feel,
76% of data professionals

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:08.440
say that this is the least
enjoyable part of their work.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.419
It's like flossing,
or maybe doing

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:16.280
the dishes it sounds like, 80%
of a data professional's time

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.620
is in data preparation.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.830
So if we were to structure
this conference like you

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:24.070
do your data time, I'd be
talking for like three days

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.455
straight and the ML team
would have like 30 minutes.

00:02:27.455 --> 00:02:29.830
And nobody wants to hear from
me for three days straight.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.750
So let's figure
out what's going on

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:35.370
and how we can fix
data preparation.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.670
First, the cast and
crew of those involved.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.680
There tends to be
kind of a spectrum

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.020
or at least camps of people.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.560
There's the business
folks who are

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.340
close to the problem,
the need for the data,

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.950
and then there are those with
the data skills, the data

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.050
scientists or engineers
as we might call them.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.500
It turns out that
all of these people

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.830
have data preparation
needs and problems.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.290
And Cloud Data Prep,
as you see today,

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:05.760
we think solves problems
for all these people.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.730
Although they do have
their common tools today,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.340
some of them are available
on Google Cloud platform

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.050
or within the G suite.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.420
So I don't mean to
discount those tools,

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:17.470
these are great tools.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.690
And if you're comfortable
with them, stick with them.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.750
In particular, if we're
talking about data preparation,

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.220
Data Studio is a wonderful
place for data scientists

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.640
in an easy to set up
and a familiar IPython

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.590
like environment.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.340
But today we're going to
talking about Data Prep.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.974
And to get you geared on, again,
why we need data preparation,

00:03:39.974 --> 00:03:41.890
I just want to start
with this kind of analogy

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:45.260
that we're going from
raw to refined data.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:49.810
People often ask, so
what is this raw data

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.250
that needs all this cleaning?

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:52.720
Well, you've seen it before.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.469
This is logs, billing
data, transactions.

00:03:57.469 --> 00:03:59.260
And the reason why
we're doing the cleaning

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.597
is because as soon as you
try and visualize this or run

00:04:01.597 --> 00:04:04.180
a report, you realize that, all
oh, there's these null values,

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:07.630
and that you'd prefer these
things in different columns

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.190
so that you could sort by them.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:11.840
And very quickly, the
need for data preparation

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.660
arises, and then you spend
80% of your time doing it.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.720
So let's use a
motivating example

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.930
throughout this that
we can refer to for why

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:24.520
we're doing this data prep.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.510
Imagine you're one of these line
of business users I refer to.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:32.530
You're more familiar with
sheets or maybe a SQL

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.480
every now and then,
and you'd like

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.190
to measure the impact and
value of your customer support

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:44.330
portal, specifically web chat
we'll say hypothetically.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:45.830
The first thing you
might have to do

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.697
is figure out where
all this data lives.

00:04:48.697 --> 00:04:50.780
And then you're going to
have to do some cleaning,

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:51.470
as we mentioned.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.060
And you're going
to realize through

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:55.143
that process you need to
structure it a little bit

00:04:55.143 --> 00:04:56.152
differently, enrich it.

00:04:56.152 --> 00:04:57.860
These are the steps
that I'm referring to

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:01.150
as data preparation.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:02.915
And, as mentioned,
you probably don't

00:05:02.915 --> 00:05:04.040
have the skills to do this.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:08.110
So if you were to try and
do this on your own today,

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.230
you might first enlist
the help of an expert.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.896
We'll call it a data
scientist, but it's

00:05:11.896 --> 00:05:14.980
somebody who can find
and understand the data

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.949
sources you're looking to use.

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:20.240
So this data scientist is
going to go through basically

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.110
that same process we
described, but they're

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.360
going to do it in a way that
you might not understand.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:27.740
This is like ninja scripts,
as they're called in Python,

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.420
they're going to use R. And
other people in the room

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.410
are going to think, oh,
R that's such a bad idea.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.119
And they're going to
use SQL, and they're

00:05:35.119 --> 00:05:37.284
going to take us-- they're
probably going to do this

00:05:37.284 --> 00:05:38.510
on a sample of the data.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:40.940
And then use on
their local machine,

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.760
and they're going to
return this to you.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.260
And you're going to say, great,
so this has all the answers.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:47.970
And they're like, actually
this is just a sample.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.370
And if you, really want
to run this every day

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.060
we're going to have to get
a data engineer involved.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:54.800
So you're already like a week
or two into this project,

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.360
and you get the data
engineer involved.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:57.660
And they do more or
less the same thing,

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.535
but this time they're
going to do it in Java,

00:05:59.535 --> 00:06:01.010
and they're going
to ad unit tests.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:02.570
And they're going
to run it on Hadoop.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.190
And they have this
whole environment set up

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:05.690
so you don't have
to worry about it.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:08.840
But now you're another two or
three weeks into the process.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.520
And people are asking
you for funding.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.970
So you're getting executive
stakeholders involved,

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.136
and maybe you don't want
to go through this in order

00:06:16.136 --> 00:06:17.157
to measure the impact.

00:06:17.157 --> 00:06:18.740
I might be a little
bit over-dramatic,

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.090
but I might not actually.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.420
So this is where we
think Data Prep can help.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:29.440
It helps you do this in
your own workstation,

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.880
on your own time, in the
moment you need to do it.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.350
So let's get in to telling
you about that first benefit

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:41.160
pillar, the visual
interactive interface.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.130
But before that, I want you
to be able to contrast this

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.050
with how we do it today.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.650
So I've done some of this
data science-y type work,

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:53.066
and I have basically a little
cocktail of queries and scripts

00:06:53.066 --> 00:06:54.690
that I like to throw
in a new data set.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.315
Because I have to understand if
this thing is exactly what it's

00:06:57.315 --> 00:07:01.200
called, how fresh it
is, and basically I'm

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.814
treating data like a black box.

00:07:02.814 --> 00:07:04.230
I don't actually
look at the data,

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:07.620
I just kind of poke and
prod it to understand it.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:08.670
This is one approach.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.580
Another, oh, I'm sorry.

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:16.470
Whereas Data Prep just throws
you right into your data.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:18.870
Rather than feeling through
a dark room for furniture,

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.340
we throw the flood lights on.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.423
And you see your data and
you can interact with it

00:07:22.423 --> 00:07:24.440
immediately.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.320
We'll take a quick tour
of some of the elements

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.940
in the user interface so
you get a feel for them.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.800
The first thing you're
probably noticing

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.780
is you're seeing distributions
along the top of your data.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:44.190
Each column is profiled to
understand the distribution

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:48.480
as presented in
histograms and in ranges.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.220
In the case of geographic
data or other data,

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:53.340
it's easily described
in different ways.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:55.710
We can throw it on a map.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.900
In the case of a time
series, we would display it

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.950
in a time series.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:05.750
You're also seeing, at the
top, a type for each column.

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.990
And you're seeing as well
a mostly green bar, maybe

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.970
a little bit of black
and red in there as well.

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:13.370
This is telling you how
well that column actually

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:14.870
fits in that type.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:18.260
So Data Prep has
detected the type,

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:21.320
and if it's all green then in
fact all the values are valid

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:22.550
along that type.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:24.960
If you're seeing some black,
or there are missing values,

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.940
red, then these are actually
invalid according to that type.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:32.450
You can adjust the
type as necessary.

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:36.904
You can also create custom types
if the 17 available immediately

00:08:36.904 --> 00:08:39.779
in Data Prep don't suit you.

00:08:39.779 --> 00:08:41.820
And there's also a series
of date and time types.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.960
So we kind of got types covered.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.140
This was the other kind
of motivating example.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:53.340
So traditional ETL
tools put you in kind

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.974
of a logic centric view.

00:08:55.974 --> 00:08:58.140
So if you want to apply
transformations to the data,

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:02.370
you typically see a block of
where your data came from,

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:04.560
a transformation, maybe a join.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.990
But at this point, towards
the end of my pipeline,

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:08.940
I don't quite know what
my data looks like,

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.860
and it's hard to have
a mental model of what

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:13.620
I need to apply to next.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:15.420
In the case of
Data Prep, we just

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.990
showed you how you have a
mental model of your data

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:23.610
when you're visualizing
the data grid.

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.760
And there it's easy to detect
what kind of transformation

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.459
you want to apply to next.

00:09:29.459 --> 00:09:31.500
You can either use the
queues that we've provided

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.210
for you in the distributions.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:37.980
You might detect an outlier
or want to transform

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:41.590
the missing or invalid values.

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:44.500
Or if you see some
other kind of anomaly,

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:47.860
all you do is just
highlight or click the data.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:50.290
And what Data Prep
begins to do is

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:53.920
suggest the transformation
it thinks you're

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:56.286
invoking by this highlight.

00:09:56.286 --> 00:09:57.910
It may take a highlight
or two in order

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:02.700
to arrive at precisely the
transformation you want.

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.690
These suggestions are
shown at the bottom.

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:07.950
And you'll see that they
have kind of sub suggestions

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.090
within a suggestion.

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.970
So you can alter the
suggestion as necessary,

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.980
and then apply it to a data set.

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.860
So you can apply one
transformation that way,

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.215
but generally you're planning
lots of transformations.

00:10:20.215 --> 00:10:21.880
A clean here, clean
a clean there.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:23.650
Some structuring, enriching.

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:27.130
These transformations are added
up into what we call a recipe.

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:30.580
It's a little more approachable
than the word script, perhaps.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:34.510
And recipes in Data Prep may
be slightly different than SQL

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.450
or Python, are human readable
or at least human readable

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:39.470
to most humans.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:43.157
And they are hidden by default.

00:10:43.157 --> 00:10:44.740
So if you're the
line of business user

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:46.240
who just wants to
make a few changes

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:48.840
and doesn't want
to learn the world,

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:51.160
it's very easy and approachable.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:52.880
But as you continue
to use Data Prep,

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.320
and if you become a power user,
it's also very expressive.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.420
You don't need to feel
like you're held back

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:00.700
by some of the
ease of Data Prep,

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:04.510
because these recipes
still behave like scripts.

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:09.910
They export, you can apply
advanced functions within them,

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:13.240
and the recipe itself
can be reordered,

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:18.370
inserted undo, rewind,
play forward, roll back.

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:22.680
It all means the same thing, I'm
just adding and adding to it.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.160
So you've explored
your data, you've

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:28.490
applied some
transformations, the data

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:31.140
is looking a lot cleaner.

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.540
But really, typically
with these projects,

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:37.950
you're trying to put the data
in a new place, in a new form.

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.170
They're still shaping
and enriching to do.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.300
And joins tend to be the
hardest part, at least

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:46.150
for the SQL newbies.

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:47.490
I'll put myself in that bucket.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:50.070
I still get a little bit
anxious what I have to do a join

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.570
and figure out the keys.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:57.280
So in Data Prep you'll be
looking at a single data set.

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:00.640
You'll choose to
add a new data set.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:02.000
You'll see a preview.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.280
So all along the way
you're kind of knowing

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:04.363
you're on the right track.

00:12:04.363 --> 00:12:06.730
I've got my original data
set, I got my preview.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:12.430
And then when you select a
join we detect common keys

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:16.270
as they're called, common fields
that are likely to be joinable.

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:20.260
And you can choose the keys that
you want to include of these,

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:22.480
and then go ahead and start
choosing all the columns.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:25.332
Really joining isn't so
much of a scary process

00:12:25.332 --> 00:12:27.790
as much or just selecting what
you want from the two tables

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:31.840
and shipping it
off as a new step

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:34.901
in your recipe of
transformations.

00:12:34.901 --> 00:12:36.650
You'll notice that
basically everything is

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:38.358
a step in your recipe
of transformations:

00:12:38.358 --> 00:12:42.650
renaming things,
joining, enriching.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:45.830
Which makes these
recipes portable,

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:48.110
because they contain
all the necessary steps,

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:51.560
including formatting in
that to shape a data set.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:57.970
So we've done that kind of
a whirlwind tour of the user

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:00.790
interface of Data
Prep to introduce you

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:03.130
to the features of how
you interact with things

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:05.890
in a visual, quick way.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:08.290
And I mentioned quick
because most of what

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.780
we do here at Google
Cloud, we pride ourselves

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:13.320
in it being very scalable.

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:18.270
So how do you quickly
navigate change columns on up

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:19.620
to petabytes of data?

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:23.940
Well, what we initially do in
Data Prep is you take a sample.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.310
You're always operating
in the browser

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:27.980
on a sample of your data.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:31.020
And We give you some control
over that sampling process,

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:34.260
so make sure it's
representative.

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:37.740
And so this next bit
is about how we then

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:41.400
take the transformations that
you've applied on your sample

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.760
and scale them out
to run on any size.

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:49.600
Now, here I draw a contrast,
not to traditional ETL tools

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:57.970
or to data science approaches
to data preparation, but in fact

00:13:57.970 --> 00:14:00.730
to other kind of standard
approaches to data preparation.

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:02.140
There are other
vendors and tools

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:06.410
out there to provide
data preparation tooling.

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:09.780
And in those cases, what we
might call first generation,

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:13.710
they empower business users to
have to apply transformations

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:15.360
themselves.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.730
But it still gets hard when
you want to scale this out.

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:20.390
You still need to hire
an IT and a data ops team

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:23.340
to make the cluster
of some kind.

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:25.350
You have now multiple
vendors, they're

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.320
providing you different
parts of the solution.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.220
So you're negotiating
org-wide licenses

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:32.640
or you have to offset costs.

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:35.610
And then finally there's
this app permissions,

00:14:35.610 --> 00:14:38.160
data permissions,
software permissions

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.170
that need to be coordinated.

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:42.980
Part of the benefit of having
this integrated in the Google

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:49.000
Cloud platform is that
the execution environment

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.070
is dead simple.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.320
And it's baked right into
all the permissioning models

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:53.860
you're used to.

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:57.520
You simply hit Run Job, and
we spin up a data flow job

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:03.140
and execute this across
the entire data set.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:05.300
So let's go into
a little bit more

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:08.090
about how data flow
does this for you.

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:09.920
Behind the scenes
these are the steps

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:12.850
that occur when a
user clicks Run Job.

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:17.080
Data Prep creates an Apache
Beam pipeline in Java

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:19.720
and submits that pipeline
through Cloud Dataflow.

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:23.320
So this is a translation from
your human, readable script

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:27.220
or recipe to a Java pipeline
that you may or may not

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:29.404
be very familiar with.

00:15:29.404 --> 00:15:30.820
Dataflow then does
what it usually

00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:34.520
does with a pipeline of this,
it detects the size and pace

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:36.610
at which it's making
progress to the data set

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:38.830
and auto scales as necessary.

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:41.440
It continues to add
resources optimized

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:44.900
for the execution of the job.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.760
The job is producing metrics,
monitoring metrics that

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.270
surfaced too a easy
to read UI that you

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:52.380
can click to if you so choose.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.830
But I can imagine many
users are happy to not

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:57.470
look at the monitoring UI.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:59.110
And as it's being
processed, which

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:03.520
is kind of unique to a data Prep
job and not every Dataflow job,

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:06.310
we're doing profiling.

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:08.020
So as I mentioned
at the beginning,

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:12.760
you saw a perhaps representative
sample of your data set.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:16.150
You saw histograms,
typing, and values

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:19.719
but these were all
histograms on the sample.

00:16:19.719 --> 00:16:22.260
So one of the things you might
be wondering after you execute

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:24.270
this over perhaps
petabytes of data

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:27.554
is that sample, the 10
megabytes, even representative?

00:16:27.554 --> 00:16:29.220
And so Data Prep is
then going to return

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:32.250
to you a profile of your data.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:35.400
So here's a sample profile.

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:40.460
You're seeing the
histogram's you saw before

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:43.830
and the typing and data
validation you saw before.

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:46.770
We're also seeing a little
bit more information, which

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:50.160
can help you, like I said, make
the decision of whether this

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:53.400
is in fact, representative,
and there are any issues.

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:56.790
If there are, you can easily
jump back into your recipe,

00:16:56.790 --> 00:17:01.760
take a new sample, or make
the necessary corrections.

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:03.300
And from this view,
as you can see,

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:08.819
we can also export
results and view a recipe.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:10.560
So you're probably
wondering where

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:14.490
this petabyte of
transformation just ended up.

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:17.849
As with the integration
with Dataflow

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:21.400
Data Prep is integrated with
BigQuery and Cloud Storage

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:28.860
as the obvious
storage solutions.

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:31.470
You can read BigQuery
tables, Cloud Storage

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:34.830
files of the expected
common types.

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:38.900
And then, of course, you can
use these similar storage

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:39.830
as targets.

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:42.420
And there's a lot of
other storage systems

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:46.020
on Google Cloud, and we'll look
to those as well in the future.

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:50.100
And then of course, there's
other non-Google source systems

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.080
that we'd love to
support in the future.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:55.710
But we're finding our
users in the private alpha

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:58.740
that we've had
previously, have had

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:01.260
no problem keeping themselves
busy with BigQuery and Cloud

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:03.030
Storage.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:05.250
And for many of
our BigQuery users,

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:09.510
this is a boon for them in
terms of database migration

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:10.858
and schema migration.

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.790
So in maybe 15 or
20 minutes we've

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:19.580
kind of covered the nuts
and bolts of Data prep, nuts

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:21.110
and bolts of Data Prep.

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:24.510
I want to go into a
demo, as promised.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:26.180
We'll spend the bulk
of our time here.

00:18:29.282 --> 00:18:31.490
Just to kind of give you
some background on the demo,

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:35.330
we mentioned earlier that
our motivating example

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:37.820
was a line of business
user who wanted

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:45.580
to measure the impact in
quality of the web chat

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:47.390
experience for their customers.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:50.610
I often hear this as like, I
want a 360 view of my customer,

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:51.990
as we mentioned earlier.

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:53.950
And I guess what
that means is that I

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:58.224
want to see what the customer
experience is like when they're

00:18:58.224 --> 00:19:00.890
learning about the product, when
they're purchasing the product,

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:02.930
when they come back for
support on the product,

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:05.471
and maybe when they're not even
interacting with the product,

00:19:05.471 --> 00:19:07.270
elsewhere in their daily lives.

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:10.140
Businesses want to understand
users in this sense,

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:13.340
and normally doing
this is difficult.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:17.110
But in what we have
here, maybe a half hour,

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:22.330
we're going to see if we can
get a 360 view of our customer

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:26.010
with the help of
Cloud Data Prep.

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:31.410
Let's imagine I'm here in my,
I don't need this anymore,

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.830
my data kind of home page.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:36.600
And we're already
seeing a few concepts

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:37.980
that may be new to you.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:42.560
So flows, data sets, and jobs
is listed across the top.

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:44.364
A flow is kind of
like a workspace.

00:19:44.364 --> 00:19:46.530
This is where I'm going to
put a collection of files

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:49.850
that I'm working on
for a certain project.

00:19:49.850 --> 00:19:51.650
Data sets are what
they sound like,

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:56.130
as well as jobs are an
execution on Dataflow

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:59.490
So we're going to create a
workspace for this project.

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:03.030
We'll call it 360 View because
we're not afraid of buzzwords.

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:10.240
And I'm going to
add some data sets.

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:12.730
Now, let's pretend
that an engineer

00:20:12.730 --> 00:20:15.130
